  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "What is the value of the following expression?\n\n\\[\\frac{2 + 4 + 6 + \\cdots + 34}{3 + 6 + 9 + \\cdots + 51}\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{17}{3}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{34}{3}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Both sums have \\(17\\) terms, because \\(34=2\\times17\\) and \\(51=3\\times17\\). Factoring out \\(2\\) from the numerator and \\(3\\) from the denominator leaves the same sum.\n\nLet \\(S=1+2+\\cdots+17\\). Then\n\\[\n\\frac{2+4+\\cdots+34}{3+6+\\cdots+51}\n=\\frac{2S}{3S}\n=\\frac23.\n\\]\nThe cancellation is valid because \\(S>0\\). Thus the value is \\(\\boxed{\\frac23}\\), choice B.",
